WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.434
-My first guest
is an Emmy-winning actor,

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:03.702
writer, and producer.

00:00:03.702 --> 00:00:06.239
He co-wrote and performs
the new audio series

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.007
called "The History
of Sketch Comedy,"

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:11.077
which is available today
exclusively on Audible.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:14.380
Here is Keegan-Michael Key!

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.384
-[ Laughs ] Jimmy Fallon!

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.820
-If there was an audience here,
they'd be going bonkers for you.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.422
Buddy, it's so great to see you.
Thank you for being here.

00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:24.791
-You, too, my friend.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.826
-Last time you were on our show,

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:29.963
we did a thing called
"Wheel of Musical Impressions."

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:32.031
-Yes.
-And you crushed it.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:35.201
And it went viral. Thank you for
doing that bit with us.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.702
I knew you could do
some impressions,

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:39.338
but I don't think everyone knew
that you could sing like that.

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.807
It was unbelievable.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.742
-Yeah. That was so much fun that
I got --

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.777
that we had the opportunity
to do that together.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:47.480
Because I really,
I love to sing.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.281
And a lot of folks
don't know that about me,

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:52.385
let alone that I could sing
in a style or styles as it were.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.621
-It was Bob Marley.
We did Michael Stipe.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.556
You did Sinatra.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:57.857
-"Sinatra, baby."

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.825
Yeah, that was -- Oh, my God.
It was so much fun.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.360
-Do you do any other
musical impressions

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.562
that we didn't touch on?

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.998
-I can do impressions
of certain musicians.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:09.202
So, I can do
an impression of Snoop.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:12.371
Oh, you know what I can do?
I can impersonate Eddie Vedder.

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:14.407
I think Eddie Vedder's
always good, 'cause it's like

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:19.278
[Mumbling Pearl Jam's
"Even Flow"]

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.080
You know, it's --
[Sings indistinctly]

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.283
I don't know any words
to any Pearl Jam songs.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:25.452
I just know --

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:30.589
[ Mumbling Pearl Jam's
"Even Flow" ]

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.892
And you can't open your eyes.
And you have to keep your eye--

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:34.593
-Yeah, you got to keep your
eyes -- and get into it.

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:37.496
-The only other thing else that
I do is, I can do Snoop Dogg,

00:01:37.496 --> 00:01:39.198
not rapping, per se.

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:42.968
But I was in "Pitch Perfect 2,"

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:44.970
and he was in it, and he and I
were in a scene together.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.673
I was playing a music producer.
And I remember he came on set,

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:48.874
and everybody was,
like, really giddy.

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.109
And he walked past me,

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:52.178
and as he was walking past me,
he looks at me,

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:55.848
because he knows that I used
to impersonate him on "Mad TV."

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:59.518
So he walked past me, and he
went, "You a bad, bad boy."

00:01:59.518 --> 00:02:02.689
[ Laughs ]

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:04.456
-Yes!

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:06.225
I love that he said that.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.828
"You're a bad, bad boy."
Oh, my gosh.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.429
-Yeah, that was it.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:12.632
-How do you impressions?

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:15.300
Like, how do you figure out
how to do them?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.871
-Frankly, the way
I get into them

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:20.039
is often by -- The way I started

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:23.743
was by listening to other people
do impressions.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:27.513
In fact, you, listening
to you and Jordan

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:29.115
and a really amazing
impressionist

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.183
named Frank Caliendo,
who was on "Mad TV" with me.

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:32.819
-Oh, he's fantastic.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.253
-Amazing, right?
-He's unbelievable.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:37.056
-For us, what was always
interesting was, it's --

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.392
It's when somebody else
found the hook,

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.027
then I could jump into the hook.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.928
So, like with --
Think about Rich Little,

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:45.064
when Rich Little used
to do President Reagan,

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.067
anybody can kind of do,
like, "that husky voice."

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.038
But what it was,
was that word "Well, well."

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.205
-It was.
-Oh, that's right,

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:54.841
The president does say "Well."

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.910
It was like, "Well, as a nation,
we are going to --"

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.445
And so, that was --

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.048
Remember that?
And so, I always used to go,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.983
that's how I would get into it
is by going,

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.386
what's the little hook
that you have to find?

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.821
-I know you have
a President Obama impression,

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.491
and you had the chance
to meet him a few times.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:14.526
Have you ever done your Obama
in front of Obama?

00:03:14.526 --> 00:03:18.297
-[ As Obama ]
"Uh, I've done it for him,

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:20.899
and, uh, a couple times, so..."

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:23.702
[ Laughs ] And it's --

00:03:23.702 --> 00:03:26.773
Last year,
I got to do it for him,

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.908
like, explicitly almost for him.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:33.045
I was telling him a story
about impersonating him.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.714
I was telling the story
about the fact

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:37.817
that I was impersonating him
to Ethel Kennedy,

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:40.219
to Robert F. Kennedy's wife.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.421
And her kids put me up to it.

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:45.491
And then, I got to relay
that story to him

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.761
and tell him that I was
doing this impersonation.

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.529
And he was giggling and laughing
at the story,

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:54.901
and then at the end, Jimmy,
he goes, "You know, um,

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.637
my register's a little bit lower
than what you're doing."

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:00.673
[ Laughs ]
-Love it!

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.509
-Duly noted, sir. Thank you.
Thank you, sir. Appreciate that.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:05.778
-I think I tried to do
an impression in front of him,

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:08.281
and he was like,
"Is that supposed to be me?"

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.549
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:12.485
You're like, I'll be over here.

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.553
-Yeah, exactly.
What am I gonna do?

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:16.021
-But how do you do it?

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:18.124
How do you hook in
to impressions?

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.859
-I just start doing people.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:22.261
I had this thing
when I was a kid, I think,

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.263
like I would watch "Rocky,"
and at the end of "Rocky,"

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:26.399
I thought that I was Rocky.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.033
So I --
-Ah, interesting.

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.935
-So I started acting like him,
"and talking like him

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:33.673
and yo, hey. Hey, Mickey,
you know," you know, whatever.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.409
I remember, like,
I watched "Seinfeld,"

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:38.143
by the end of the episode
I was talking,

00:04:38.143 --> 00:04:40.212
[As Jerry Seinfeld]
"Everything was like that!

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.914
I was doing that.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.450
I was trying to be him," and --

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.252
-It's -- You're so good at it.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.654
I mean, I've always enjoyed

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.423
your Dave Matthews
and your Robin Williams.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:52.625
Those are two that have always,
like, blown me out of the water.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:56.395
I'm just like, how is he --
Where did he find that rhythm?

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.763
It's just so good.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:01.200
-This is so exciting.
"The History of Sketch Comedy,"

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.236
how did this whole thing
come about?

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:06.238
-So, basically, El,
you know my wife, El.

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:07.339
-Of course, I love El.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:09.375
-Yeah, my wife El and I,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:12.345
we have always shared
this love of comedy.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:15.281
And during quarantine,
she suggested

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:17.850
that we put our combined
knowledge together

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.253
and then try to create, you
know, something for everyone.

00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:22.955
And this is
what we came up with.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:25.224
It happens that, you know,
it's what happens

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:29.362
when you live with a DGA
director and a PGA producer

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:31.830
and a brilliant writer.
So I just had the opportunity.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.899
She did it all.
She was just like,

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:36.468
"Hey, you know more about sketch
comedy than anybody I know.

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:39.004
Let's smack our knowledge
together and do this thing."

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:40.540
And I was like, "Yeah,
that sounds like a good idea."

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.143
And then she -- She kind of
created this format,

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:47.112
and we started doing
all the research.

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:49.681
And then we pitched it
to Audible.

00:05:49.681 --> 00:05:51.617
And it was amazing,
because the way she pitched it,

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.085
she was like,
"What about this, guys?"

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:58.691
She was like, "If Keegan-Michael
Key was a guest lecturer at NYU

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.294
and was going to give
a 10-part class

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.929
on the history of sketch comedy,

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:04.564
I think that would be
very popular.

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:07.300
I think he would get a lot of
apples at the end of the class."

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.834
And then they bought it.
-Good pitch.

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:10.503
-That was her pitch.
That was her pitch.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:13.139
-It's a good pitch.
-And then, she was --

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.907
So we started doing it,
and we're doing the research,

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:16.175
which of course
was a blast for me.

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.911
I love -- I'm a nerd
about this kind of stuff.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.246
And she goes --
I said, "You know, it's fun,

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:22.615
because we'll be able
to get the clips

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:24.149
and do this and that
and the other thing."

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:25.818
And El goes, "Oh, no, no, no,
there's no clips.

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.053
You're going to be performing
all the characters,

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:29.521
and you're going to be doing
all the sketches."

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.057
I was like, "I'm sorry,
I'm going to be doing what, now?

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:33.392
I'm doing all the what
to the who-who's?"

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:35.761
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:38.130
-Wow.
-But that is what we did.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.766
-You stepped it up.
-So I do all the foley,

00:06:40.766 --> 00:06:42.834
all the sound effects,
all the characters.

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:44.403
I'll do two-person scenes,
three-person scenes.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:46.372
I'll play all three people
in the scene.

00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:50.242
And then, in the middle of it,
I'll, like, analyze it.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:52.178
So sometimes
there will be like a sketch,

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:54.246
like, there will be a guy
saying, you know,

00:06:54.246 --> 00:06:56.215
from the Marx Brothers,
there will be somebody saying,

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:58.751
you know, "We have to stop war

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.920
because it's prohibitive
for our taxes!"

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.689
And then Chico Marx
will be like,

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:06.459
"Hey, I got a uncle
who lives in Taxes."

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:10.429
"No! Not Texas.
Dollar, money, dollars!"

00:07:10.429 --> 00:07:13.832
"Ha-ha! That's where he lives.
Dallas, Taxes."

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:15.835
[ Mumbling as Chico Marx ]

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:17.736
You know?

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:20.405
-Dude, there's some Marx
Brothers bits that I honestly,

00:07:20.405 --> 00:07:23.609
I have to just pause it or turn
it off 'cause I'm laughing --

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:26.478
It's too funny.
There's levels of funny.

00:07:26.478 --> 00:07:29.215
-Jimmy, it's crazy.
We start at like the beginning.

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:31.383
I mean, I think
some people might go,

00:07:31.383 --> 00:07:33.352
"Oh, he's going to do a history
of the last 30 years.

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:36.288
He's going to -- You know, maybe
he's going to start with 'SNL.'"

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:39.258
But I start with
the ancient Sumerians

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:41.660
from the 19th century BC

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.096
and work all the way up
to today. So --

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.632
-That's wild.
But, I mean, like, you go, yeah.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:49.368
I mean, you do touch on "SNL."

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:51.003
Are there any sketches.
now that it's done,

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.773
that you go, "Ah, we should have
talked about that."

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:56.142
Or are there any performers --
Let's not even say performers --

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:59.812
any performer
that you might have --

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:02.314
-[ Laughing ]

00:08:02.314 --> 00:08:03.749
No, Jimmy.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:06.785
No, there is no performer
that I forgot,

00:08:06.785 --> 00:08:08.287
because you're in it.

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:10.756
You're in the Audible series.
-Where?

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.992
-Jimmy, Camp Winnipesaukee,

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.560
with you and me
and Justin Timberlake.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:15.961
-That's right, bud.
-And Billy Crystal.

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.164
-Oh, that's right!
-You are in it.

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:20.199
I talk about you,
and there's a whole section

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:22.969
about variety entertainment
and talk shows. You're in it.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:25.404
-That's why, everyone,
get out there right now.

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:29.441
Go to Audible, download
"The History of Sketch Comedy."

00:08:29.441 --> 00:08:32.077
Keegan-Michael Key,
you are the best, buddy.

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:33.412
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:34.780
-Thanks, Jimmy. Good to see you.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.182
-Good to see you, buddy.
-Jimmy Fallon is in this series!

00:08:37.182 --> 00:08:38.217
Listen --
-We'll be right back.

